= Explanation — Technical =

== Deductive-nomological model ==
Hempel and Oppenheim modeled explanation as a valid deduction:

<pre>
L1, L2, ... Ln
C1, C2, ... Cn
----------------
E
</pre>

where L are general laws, C are conditions, and E is the explanandum.

Challenges include:
* explanatory asymmetry;
* irrelevant premises;
* probabilistic phenomena;
* laws with no causal relevance.

== Causal models ==
Causal explanations can be represented through structural equations:

<pre>
Y := f(X, U)
</pre>

Intervention changes in X support counterfactual questions about Y.

A causal model explains only relative to its represented variables and [[System boundary/Technical|system boundary]].

== Mechanistic explanations ==
Mechanistic analysis decomposes phenomena into entities and activities organized across levels.

Technical adequacy can depend on:
* productive continuity;
* localization;
* temporal order;
* component interactions;
* intervention evidence.

Mechanistic depth is not always better: explanation is constrained by the question and useful scale.

== Unification ==
Unificationist accounts value derivation of many phenomena using a small number of argument patterns.

A unified account can explain even without detailed mechanism, but critics argue that unification alone can select explanatorily irrelevant regularities.

== Statistical explanation ==
For stochastic systems, explanation may concern:
* probability distributions;
* generative processes;
* hazard/risk;
* population-level causal effects;
* expected outcomes.

An individual event can remain improbable even when generated by a well-explained stochastic process.

== Contrastive explanation ==
Many why-questions have the form:

<pre>
Why P rather than Q?
</pre>

The contrast Q changes which causes or factors are relevant.

This helps explain why the same event can support multiple correct explanations at different levels.

== Explanatory model selection ==
Explanatory virtues can include:
* fit to evidence;
* causal/mechanistic adequacy;
* simplicity;
* unification;
* robustness;
* counterfactual support;
* coherence with established knowledge.

These virtues can conflict and should not be compressed into one universal score.

